{
  "id": "verb-position-2",
  "name": "Word order: subject, verb, object",
  "level": "A1",
  "subtitle": "In English the order is fixed: subject, then verb, then object: Amira likes music. Put the place before the time: We go to school at eight.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "subject",
    "verb",
    "object",
    "place before the time"
  ],
  "source": "EnglishAcademy original English text (grammar-a1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "status": "entwurf",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "subject"
      },
      {
        "text": "verb",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "object"
      },
      {
        "text": "place · time"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "Mei <span class=\"vp-verb\">drinks</span> tea. — We <span class=\"vp-verb\">go</span> to school <span class=\"vp-prep\">at eight</span>.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "A basic sentence has a <strong>subject</strong> (who or what does it), a <strong>verb</strong> (the action) and often an <strong>object</strong> (who or what the action is for): <em>Amira likes music. Ben drinks tea.</em> The order is always <strong>subject – verb – object</strong>.",
        "tag": "S–V–O"
      },
      {
        "html": "English sentences always need a <strong>subject</strong>, and it comes <strong>before the verb</strong>: ✗ <em>Is cold today.</em> → ✓ <em>It is cold today.</em> Nothing goes between the verb and its object: ✗ <em>I like very much music.</em> → ✓ <em>I like music very much.</em>",
        "tag": "subject first"
      },
      {
        "html": "If a sentence has a place and a time, put the <strong>place first</strong> and the <strong>time second</strong>: <em>I go to work at eight. We play football in the park on Sunday.</em> Not ✗ <em>I go at eight to work.</em>",
        "tag": "place before time"
      },
      {
        "html": "Words like <strong>always, usually, often, never</strong> go <strong>before the main verb</strong>: <em>I always drink tea.</em> After <strong>be</strong>, they go after the verb: <em>She is never late.</em> An <strong>adjective</strong> goes before the noun: <em>a big dog</em>, not ✗ <em>a dog big</em>.",
        "tag": "always, adjectives"
      },
      {
        "html": "In <strong>questions</strong>, <strong>do / does</strong> or a form of <strong>be</strong> comes before the subject: <em>Do you like tea? Is she a doctor? Where does he work?</em>",
        "tag": "questions"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "You can also put the time first: <em>On Monday I work in Leeds.</em> The rest of the order stays the same. Learn the verb forms in <a href=\"/praesens\">Present simple</a>. Questions have their own pages: <a href=\"/w-fragen\">Wh- questions</a> and <a href=\"/ja-nein-fragen\">Yes/no questions</a>. Commands start with the verb: <a href=\"/imperativ\">Imperatives</a>. To join two sentences, see <a href=\"/aduso\">Linking words</a>.",
    "reference": {
